\title{Sets as theories}
\import{prelude}

\p{A model \code{A : Set} may itself be used as a theory. A model of \code{A} as a theory is just an element of \code{A} (remember that a model of \code{Set} is a collection of things). For example, consider the following theory.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} PopulatedSantaList := \kw{sig}
  list : SantaList
  john : list.nice
  mary : list.naughty
\kw{end}
}

\p{A model \code{m} of \code{PopulatedSantaList} consists of a model \code{m.list} of \code{SantaList}, along with specific elements \code{m.john} in the collection \code{m.list.nice} and \code{m.mary} in the collection \code{m.list.naughty}.}

